Author: <a href="https://www.smspower.org:443/forums/profile.php?mode=viewprofile&u=35154" target="_blank">nothingsp</a><br />

Posted: Sat Oct 31, 2026 5:13 pm (GMT 0)<br />
<br /><blockquote>  <div class="attribution">Maxim wrote    <script type="text/javascript" src="./templates/select_expand_bbcodes.js"></script>    <script type="text/javascript">    
<!--
        var id = 'SXBB' + (1000 + Math.floor(Math.random() * 5000));        SXBB[id] = new _SXBB(id);        SXBB[id].T['select'] = 'Select';        SXBB[id].T['expand'] = '[+]';        SXBB[id].T['contract'] = '[-]';        SXBB[id].writeCmd();    
//-->
    </script>  </div>  <div class="body">    <script type="text/javascript">      
<!--
        SXBB[id].writeDiv();      
//-->
    </script>Another approach that I haven’t seen much is a fizzle or ordered dither “fade” of the tile data itself. That’s a lot harder to do from black than to it :) but it does have 64 steps (if you do it tile-wise) which you can run at a high frame rate. It might be interesting to try a Wolfenstein style LFSR fizzle across the whole tileset at a fairly high speed.    <script type="text/javascript">      
<!--
        document.write('</div>');      
//-->
    </script>  </div></blockquote>
<br />
Yeah that would take more doing, but it's a pretty good alternative when your colordepth is limited.
<br />

<br />
You might also &quot;fizzle&quot; on a per-tile basis rather than per-pixel, much less demanding of VRAM bandwidth and doesn't run into issues with tile mirroring.<br />

